course: dictionary
lesson: 5643
title: Linseed and linseed oil
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Linseed is a noun. It is the seed of the flax plant, so it is also called flaxseed. You can work out what a word means by reading the words around it. This is its context.
explain: Read this: «Oil paint is pigment mixed with oil, often linseed oil; it dries slowly, so painters could blend smoothly.» The words "mixed with oil" and "painters" are clues that linseed oil goes into paint. Try your meaning in the sentence: "often oil pressed from flax seeds" still makes sense, so your meaning fits.
explain: Be careful: linseed and linseed oil are not the same. Linseed is the seed. Linseed oil is the oil pressed from those seeds. Check which one the sentence means before you answer.
fact: Linseed is the seed of the flax plant, also called flaxseed.
fact: Linseed oil is oil pressed from linseeds. It is used in paints and to protect wood.
fact: Food-grade linseed oil is made to be safe for cooking.
fact: Linseed is sometimes added to bread and cereals.
fact: Rags soaked in linseed oil can catch fire by themselves if they are crumpled up, so dry them flat.
exercise: In «Oil paint is pigment mixed with oil, often linseed oil», what is linseed oil? | Pigment, the coloured powder in paint | *Oil pressed from linseeds | A type of cloth made from flax | A kind of paintbrush
exercise: What is another name for linseed? | Cottonseed | Ryeseed | Sunflower oil | *Flaxseed
exercise: Why should a rag soaked in linseed oil not be left crumpled up? | It will become too clean to use | *It can catch fire by itself | It will turn into linen cloth | It will freeze solid
test: Which cloth does the flax plant give us? | *Linen | Silk | Denim | Wool
test: Which of these uses linseed oil? | Hot chocolate | *Oil paint | Glass windows | Pencil lead
exam: Why does the painter in «it dries slowly, so painters could blend smoothly» benefit from oil paint? | Because it dries in one second | Because it is made from flax cloth | Because it is cheap to buy | *Because they have time to blend the colours smoothly
